    "result_text": "All tests pass. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation keyed on the `Idempotency-Key` header, following the project's OpenAPI-first, Spring conventions.\n\n**1. API contract** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`) \u2014 declared `Idempotency-Key` as an optional header parameter on `POST /owners` and documented the `200` response for the \"already existed\" case. The generator produces the `@RequestHeader` binding, so the header is a first-class part of the contract rather than read ad-hoc.\n\n**2. New component** (`rest/controller/IdempotencyKeyRegistry.java`) \u2014 a small single-responsibility bean that remembers which owner id a key first created (`ConcurrentHashMap`, `putIfAbsent`). It treats null/blank keys as absent, so all the key semantics live in one place instead of leaking into the controller.\n\n**3. Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) \u2014 injected the registry and added two lines of orchestration:\n- At the top: if the key maps to an existing owner, return that owner with `200 OK` (no duplicate created).\n- After a successful save: `remember(idempotencyKey, owner.getId())`.\n\nThe heavy validation/creation logic is untouched, and no unrelated logic accumulated in the controller \u2014 the idempotency bookkeeping is delegated to the registry.\n\nA repeated create with a seen key now returns the original owner with `200` (the acceptance test `coreIdempotentRepeat` asserts `id1 == id2`), while a fresh key or an unkeyed request still creates and returns `201`.",
